@startuml
' The life of one secret.
title Secret state machine

skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultFontName Sans

[*] --> CREATED : PUT on a name that does not exist\n(role must permit creation:\nadmin or developer only)

state CREATED {
  CREATED : version = 1
  CREATED : owner = creating uid
  CREATED : fresh random nonce
  CREATED : acl_count = 0
}

state ACTIVE {
  ACTIVE : readable by owner, admin,
  ACTIVE : and whatever the ACL allows
}

state ROTATED {
  ROTATED : same plaintext
  ROTATED : new nonce and tag
  ROTATED : version++
}

CREATED --> ACTIVE : first successful GET\nor any ACL change

ACTIVE --> ACTIVE : PUT (requires WRITE)\nnew nonce, version++
ACTIVE --> ROTATED : ROTATE (requires WRITE)
ROTATED --> ACTIVE : subsequent access

ACTIVE --> ACTIVE : GRANT / REVOKE\n(requires GRANT on this secret)

ACTIVE --> REVOKED : REVOKE removes the last\nACL entry a principal relied on
REVOKED --> ACTIVE : GRANT again

ACTIVE --> [*] : DELETE (requires DELETE)\n**kfree_sensitive: zeroed before freed**
ROTATED --> [*] : DELETE
REVOKED --> [*] : DELETE

note as N1
  A nonce is never reused under one key.
  Reuse does not weaken GCM, it collapses
  it: the XOR of the two plaintexts and
  the authentication key both leak. So
  every PUT and ROTATE draws a new nonce
  from get_random_bytes() - never from a
  counter, which restoring a backup could
  rewind.
end note

note as N2
  REVOKED is a view, not a stored flag:
  the secret's state is its ACL, and
  "revoked" means no entry currently
  grants the principal in question.
end note
@enduml
